@@ -0,0 +1,75 @@
+# Deleting a whole repository
+
+This procedure removes a retired image's repository from `registry:5000`, so that it drops out of
+`/v2/_catalog`. The operator runs it.
+
+The registry API cannot delete a repository. `DELETE /v2/<repo>/manifests/<digest>` removes one
+manifest, and with it every tag that points at that manifest. The repository's directory stays on
+disk, and the catalog is a walk of those directories. So there are two steps: delete the tags
+through the API, then remove the directory from storage.
+
+## The registry as deployed
+
+RegistryDeploy's chart (`chart/templates/registry-deployment.yaml`) deploys the registry:
+
+- The image is upstream `registry` (Distribution v3), and its config file is
+  `/etc/distribution/config.yml`.
+- Storage is the filesystem, on the `registry-pvc` PVC mounted at `/var/lib/registry`. A
+  repository's directory is `/var/lib/registry/docker/registry/v2/repositories/<repo>`.
+- The namespace is `registry-prd`, and the pod carries the label `app=registry`.
+- Manifest deletes are enabled. The chart sets no delete option, but registry-cleanup's
+  `DELETE`s return `202`.
+
+## Before you start
+
+Nothing may build, push or pull the repository any more:
+
+- Its image directory is gone from DockerImages' `main`. A push to the name recreates the
+  repository.
+- No pipeline, deploy repo or other image references it.
+
+## 1. Delete every tag through the API
+
+```sh
+REG=http://registry:5000
+REPO=<repo>
+ACCEPT='application/vnd.oci.image.index.v1+json, application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.list.v2+json, application/vnd.oci.image.manifest.v1+json, application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.v2+json'
+
+curl -fsS "$REG/v2/$REPO/tags/list" | jq -r '.tags[]?' |
+  while read -r tag; do
+    curl -fsSI -H "Accept: $ACCEPT" "$REG/v2/$REPO/manifests/$tag" |
+      tr -d '\r' | awk -F': ' 'tolower($1) == "docker-content-digest" { print $2 }'
+  done | sort -u |
+  while read -r digest; do
+    curl -sS -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code} $digest\n" -X DELETE "$REG/v2/$REPO/manifests/$digest"
+  done
+```
+
+Every line must start with `202`. Tags that share a digest, such as `latest` and the build it
+points at, all go with one `DELETE`, which is why the digests are passed through `sort -u`. When
+it is done, `curl -s "$REG/v2/$REPO/tags/list"` lists no tags.
+
+## 2. Remove the repository from storage
+
+This step needs `pods/exec` in `registry-prd`, which only the prd-write kubeconfig grants:
+
+```sh
+KC="kubectl --kubeconfig $HOME/.kube/config-prd-write --context prd -n registry-prd"
+POD=$($KC get pods -l app=registry -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')
+$KC exec "$POD" -- rm -rf "/var/lib/registry/docker/registry/v2/repositories/${REPO:?set REPO to the repository}"
+```
+
+## 3. Check the catalog
+
+```sh
+curl -fsS "$REG/v2/_catalog?n=1000" | jq -r '.repositories[]' | grep -x "${REPO:?set REPO to the repository}"
+```
+
+This must print nothing. Use `n=1000`, because the unpaged catalog stops at 100 entries.
+
+## No garbage collect
+
+Do not run a garbage collect for this. The repository's blobs stay on the PVC until the next
+`registry garbage-collect`, and registry-cleanup runs that after its tag pass. registry-cleanup's
+CronJob has been suspended since RegistryDeploy `5ccb234`, until it respects what the deploy repos
+pin.
